<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>After the evacuation of more than 330,000 Allied soldiers from Dunkirk, Prime Minister Winston Churchill reported to Parliament on a disaster turned into a deliverance, and prepared the country for a possible invasion.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>We shall fight on the beaches... we shall never surrender.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Speech to the House of Commons, 4 June 1940. Quoted briefly: the full text is still under copyright.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://api.parliament.uk/historic-hansard/commons/1940/jun/04/war-situation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Hansard, UK Parliament</span><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“We shall fight on the beaches... we shall never surrender.” — Winston Churchill, Churchill: We Shall Fight on the Beaches" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-churchill-we-shall-fight.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>France fell weeks later, but Britain fought on alone through the Battle of Britain that summer and the Blitz that followed. The speech became one of the defining statements of resistance in the Second World War.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain">Battle of Britain</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-blitz">The Blitz</a></div></div></section>
